## How to cut waves
- Cut along REAL dependencies: a wave must be buildable and verifiable using only
  earlier waves. Prefer 3–12 waves; each should fit in one to three builder sessions.
- Every acceptance criterion must be checkable by running something. "Code is clean"
  is not a criterion; "`go vet ./...` exits 0" is.
- The last wave's criteria define the finish line of the whole plan.
- Gate = fast, deterministic checks (build, lint, unit tests). Fingerprints = read-only
  samples of anything outside git (databases, external dirs) that must not change.
- Set "browser" only if the plan changes something a person looks at. It gives both
  models a real browser: they load the routes, screenshot them at each viewport and
  read the console. Pick a PORT NOTHING ELSE USES — the build runs beside a developer
  who already has the usual one. List only the routes this plan touches.
- A browser does not lower the bar for acceptance criteria. "Renders correctly" is
  still not checkable; "/invoices at 390px has no horizontal overflow and a clean
  console" is, because the reviewer can load it and see for itself.
